# Python in Excel time series functions

Time series functions operate on an ordered series (`expsmooth`, `stl`, `stl_plot`, `resid_analysis`, `arima_order`, `zscore_replace`, forecasting, seasonal decomposition, and similar). `describe`, `corr`, `normality_check`, `cluster_prep`, and sampling functions are not time series.

## Init files

Copy the `def` and quoted call into **only**:

1. @source/python-in-excel/init/TimeSeries.py (time-series-only Initialization)

Do **not** also add time series functions to @source/python-in-excel/init/PaulPythonLibrary.py. Paul is the **general** init for functions that do not belong in a specialized collection. Do not add time series functions to @source/python-in-excel/init/DefaultInitialization.py.

Keep function bodies in `functions/name.py` and `TimeSeries.py` identical. `TimeSeries.py` is a complete Initialization: Excel default imports, then time series `def`s only (including shared helpers such as `stl_fit`).

Add a `contents()` row in `TimeSeries.py` in the same change. See @.cursor/rules/python-in-excel-init-contents.mdc.

```python
# ❌ BAD — time series function also copied into PaulPythonLibrary.py
# ❌ BAD — def added, contents() not updated
# ✅ GOOD — same def in functions/name.py and TimeSeries.py only; contents() row with call signature
```

Then follow @.cursor/rules/python-in-excel.mdc (function file, PythonMap, README, CHANGELOG, tests, docs).
